WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.900
- This is so cute.

00:00:02.328 --> 00:00:04.230
- Why thank you.

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.270
You had no idea what happened to me today.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.580
I got up because I was so excited

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.340
that I thought I had a little
bit of a holiday from work.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:12.173
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:12.173 --> 00:00:13.800
- [Marlee] Until, you know, whenever it is

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.870
that feeling you have some time off.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:17.430
And I got an appointment

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:20.700
for a blow dry at the dry bar at the mall.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:21.840
You know, everybody goes to the mall

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.020
and I'm there at the mall
and suddenly get a call

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:26.760
come to Kelly Clarkson right now.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:27.600
I had no clothes.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:28.890
I had nothing to wear.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:30.270
So I had to go into Nordstrom.

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:31.710
I had to grab some stuff off the racks.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:33.150
I got some socks, I got a bra.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:34.440
I got everything.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:35.273
And here I am.

00:00:35.273 --> 00:00:36.300
I feel like Cinderella

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.130
- I Dream of Jeanie. That was awesome.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:45.150
- This is a crazy time
because people are just

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:46.470
dropping left or right of Covid.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:48.510
So it's like last minute
we'll be like, okay.

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:51.150
And so we'll ask our friends that we love

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:52.830
cause we love you so much.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:54.210
So we are excited you could come here.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.040
We asked for you to come.
- [Marlee] Anything for you.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.510
- [Kelly] I love you.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.760
And I just have a quick question.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.280
Did you do the triple
points thing at Nordstrom?

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:03.113
Since you were there.

00:01:03.113 --> 00:01:04.200
- I didn't even know about that.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.033
- [Kelly] Oh

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:08.440
- I'm gonna go back right
after this. Right after this,

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:09.330
and get my triple points.
- [Kelly] You missed out

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:10.730
on a deal is all I'm saying.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:12.483
So also everybody please
welcome Marley's interpreter

00:01:12.483 --> 00:01:16.867
and producing partner.
Jack Jason, come on ya'll.

00:01:16.867 --> 00:01:17.700
[Applause]

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:22.020
- Wait, you were just talking about Covid?

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:23.944
- Yeah
- You know I got tested,

00:01:23.944 --> 00:01:24.777
I mean the guy came in
to blow my hair and then

00:01:24.777 --> 00:01:27.430
I had to get tested while I
was getting my hair blown.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:29.790
- It's a twofer

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:30.930
- Boom was like, what is she doing?

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:32.640
She's getting tested
while I'm doing her hair?

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.580
- It's so funny how we've all just

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.400
become accustomed to strange people being

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.200
in our nostrils and everything.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.220
Like in your home.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.500
Like it's just insane.
- It's the new normal.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:44.333
What are you gonna say?
- It is.

00:01:44.333 --> 00:01:46.980
Yeah, my kids know no other
world cause they're so young.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:51.900
But I wanna say congrats on
the historic win for her movie

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.327
CODA. Last year,

00:01:53.327 --> 00:01:54.902
[Cheering]

00:01:54.902 --> 00:01:57.235
we were so stoked for y'all.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:00.180
We had the whole cast here.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:01.013
I fell in love

00:02:01.013 --> 00:02:03.300
with them all over again,
even after the movie.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.230
It was so beautiful.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.510
What did y'all do that night?

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:07.890
- Well we went, let's see, I think right

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:12.060
after we went to the governor's
ball, to the parties there

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.160
to the Vanity Fair, people
were left and right.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.440
I mean usually people don't
come up to me cause they're shy.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:17.640
They think I'm deaf and I can't talk.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:19.080
But they came right up to me and they

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.930
they said like this, you
know, they deaf applause.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:23.460
And then we went to Vanity Fair.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.320
I mean my bedtime is nine o'clock

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:27.540
8:30, 9 o'clock and I
was up till one and two.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:29.790
And I was like, okay guys, I'm outta here.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:32.580
- I, yeah, but your CODA co-star

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.070
Daniel Durant competed in
Dancing with the Stars.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:36.450
So that was last season.

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.940
Did you ever get a chance to go watch him?

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:40.080
- I absolutely did.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:41.970
I was there, me and Troy Kotzer

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:44.610
we were like the proud onscreen parents

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:48.180
for Daniel visiting our son
on Dancing With the Stars.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:52.734
And we rooted him on, and
he is such a great dancer.

00:02:52.734 --> 00:02:55.290
He's in a different place here, you know

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:57.120
watching him dance as opposed to acting.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:58.890
He totally outside of the box.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:00.380
We were so proud of him.
We were so proud of him.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:01.590
- Do you like dancing?

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:03.540
I love dancing.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:06.690
- I like dancing, but I don't know

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:08.400
about being in front of 25 million people

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.970
and then having them
vote for me about that.

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:11.664
I don't know if I like that.
- No, no, no.

00:03:11.664 --> 00:03:12.990
I just meant dancing in general.

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:14.670
I think it feels so good.
- Yeah!

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:15.503
Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:15.503 --> 00:03:17.520
I loved to dance. Anytime,
anywhere, anybody.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.190
- Oh, you know she didn't even have a bar.

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:22.980
Anybody. That's amazing.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:24.450
So wait, this is important.

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:25.710
You've joined the board of Governors

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:27.150
at the Academy since we last saw you.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.493
So what exactly does it
mean to be a governor?

00:03:30.355 --> 00:03:34.260
- I joined the board of
the Academy with, you know

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:35.430
the people give out the Oscars.

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:36.960
So now I'm there.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.810
I mean, it's part of, in
addition to what I do as an actor

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:43.260
and we're involved with changing
rules, making new decisions

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.930
and making noise about
what we need to do and to

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:48.570
approve inclusivity, how
to make things better

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:49.500
at the Academy.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:53.130
And they've been nothing but
receptive, very supportive

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:55.608
of my community of what we need.

00:03:55.608 --> 00:03:57.750
I mean, if we go to any of
their events, they always

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:00.330
have interpreters there or
any of the videos they show

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:01.860
they provide them with captions.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:02.970
So I'm really honored to be

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:04.380
on the board of Governors at the Academy.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:06.243
- It's so important. Yeah.

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:09.690
It's so important. And I
think people know, you know

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:12.330
even recognize it more
so now because of CODA.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.250
Like because of a beautiful piece

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:16.573
of art that touched so many people

00:04:16.573 --> 00:04:18.420
that that is what changed
it, that domino effect.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.820
So CODA brought much needed attention

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:22.290
like I said, to deaf accessibility.

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:25.743
So what's something that you
still hope to see improved?

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:32.120
- I still hope to see more,
I guess stories having to do

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.250
with deaf characters with our culture.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.660
More deaf actors, directors, writers

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:41.250
deaf people behind the cameras working

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:43.440
as in crew members in our industry.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:44.880
I wanna see more of that.

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:45.990
- You know, what's interesting about that?

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.750
Because you know, they're
gonna have a different take

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:50.340
on it behind the camera
of what you see, how

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:53.250
they experience a film and how
they experience performance.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:56.414
That's so important to
have somebody on stage.

00:04:56.414 --> 00:04:58.036
- Absolutely. Absolutely. Absolutely.

00:04:58.036 --> 00:04:59.100
It is.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:00.600
- What can you tell us about the new show?

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.880
This is Accused, right?

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:07.710
- So this is something that
I've always wanted to do

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:09.450
for the longest time.

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:12.120
And I flirted with this idea
of doing something different

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:14.760
than what I've been doing,
which is now directing.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:16.890
And I directed episodic television

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:20.130
a new show called
Accused for Fox and Sony.

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:22.380
And it's an anthology series, which means

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:25.230
that each episode is like a little movie

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:27.360
and every week there's a different story.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:30.900
It's a great show and I'm
really excited and particularly

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:33.810
because it's my episode
and I've directed it.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:35.133
- I love that.

00:05:36.843 --> 00:05:38.640
Have you always wanted to direct

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:40.140
have you always wanted to
do stuff behind the scenes?

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:44.100
- Yeah, I've always wanted
to direct because I, you know

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.410
I have deaf eyes, so
there's something on top

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:47.520
of what everybody else has.
- Yeah.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:48.353
- I'm more visual.

00:05:48.353 --> 00:05:51.540
I'm always watching things
and how things work on a set.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:53.790
And now that I'm behind
the camera and working

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:56.100
with actors, it's so much fun for me.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.980
I mean, I get to work
with actors one-on-one

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:01.740
watching how they develop
their characters, watching how

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:03.840
they express themselves.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:06.960
And you know, as an actor,
that was a lot of fun to

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:07.793
be able to watch them.

00:06:07.793 --> 00:06:10.800
And I had deaf characters
and deaf actors to work with.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:12.780
There was no barriers with hearing actors.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:13.613
It's great. Of course.

00:06:13.613 --> 00:06:16.080
Naturally I had
interpreters and the set was

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:17.790
filled with interpreters
everywhere left and right

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:19.890
and it was very accessible.

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:24.300
But in terms of working with
the hearing actors, clearly

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.850
I used an, an interpreter
who was from Canada

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:28.740
and I love Canada by the way.

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:31.080
But she was so polite as Canadians are

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:33.180
and she's a good friend of
mine. Her name is Susie.

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:36.150
I'm calling her out and she was so nice.

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:38.790
But when I was really animated
and I wanted to get really

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:40.830
into the script, I was
looking at her and she was

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:43.440
like talking so politely and
talking with her soft voice.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:45.240
I'm like, wait a minute, I
can't use that interpreter.

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:47.040
I just decided to just speak myself.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:48.178
And that's what I did.

00:06:48.178 --> 00:06:49.170
- You needed the passion behind it.

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:50.550
- Absolutely got into it.

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:52.170
But yeah, it's an interesting
process and it was

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:53.280
an interesting journey for me.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:56.250
And being the first deaf member

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:59.419
of the Director's Guild
of America is amazing.

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:03.600
- What a cool thing.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:05.370
- That means what? That means what?

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:06.263
Open doors

00:07:06.263 --> 00:07:07.923
for other deaf directors.
- Opening doors.

00:07:07.923 --> 00:07:09.201
Yeah.

00:07:09.201 --> 00:07:10.034
Which is so important.

00:07:10.034 --> 00:07:11.850
And, and what is your
episode that you directed?

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:12.683
What's it about?

00:07:12.683 --> 00:07:13.980
What is it kind of entail?

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:17.550
- My episode is about a deaf
woman who has decided to

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:18.453
be a surrogate.

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:21.720
And it's for a woman who is hearing.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:23.130
And it just so happens

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:26.430
that after the baby is
born, it's born deaf

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:28.050
but it has nothing to
do with her being deaf.

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:29.850
And we see the journey

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:32.610
and the dynamics that go,
that she goes through.

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:35.640
The show is, it starts with something

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:37.830
about a courtroom, but you don't
know what's going to happen

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:39.330
and what the person is accused of.

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:41.400
And so the whole episode plays out

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.530
and you can see what
happens along their journey.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:44.700
- Good tease.

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:45.930
That was a nice tease.

00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:48.150
Okay, so I have to bring
this up because like me

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:50.670
you're a fan of tattoos, which
we've talked about before

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:53.370
but there's one that
means a lot to you, right?

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:56.040
- So I'm looking at the sunflowers here.

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:57.270
- Yes.

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:59.160
- And it's making me think of my mom.

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:01.530
I have her sunflower on my shoulder.

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:03.870
So she had a sunflower tattoo
and I have a sunflower tattoo.

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:04.740
That's my mom there?

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:07.200
- I have a sunflower tattoo.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:08.073
Right here.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:09.953
- There's mine.

00:08:09.953 --> 00:08:11.547
- I love sunflowers.

00:08:11.547 --> 00:08:14.490
Oh. Oh, that's beautiful.

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:15.330
That's really beautiful.

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:16.290
I love that. It's so funny.

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:17.700
I have a sunflower tattoo and I also

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:20.850
my mother was against tattoos, you know

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:22.110
kind of religiously like growing up.

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:24.420
Your body's a temple, you're
not supposed to do that.

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:25.253
Oh well.

00:08:25.253 --> 00:08:29.340
And 20 later, my temple, it's my design.

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:31.230
So we convinced my sister

00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:34.080
and I convinced her to do a
tattoo finally, like one year.

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:35.850
And she ended up getting

00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:38.820
she's the sun and I'm the
moon, which I have that tattoo

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:40.950
and my sister has a star and
we're both inside the sun.

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:44.614
So it's like, it can be a
really cool thing. Yeah.

00:08:44.614 --> 00:08:48.180
- Because my mom had one and
I got it for her birthday.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:50.640
You know, she's Jewish
and Jews don't do tattoos.

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:52.306
- Yeah.
- But I think I got it

00:08:52.306 --> 00:08:54.270
for her 65th birthday I think it was.

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:59.190
And then unfortunately years
later, she had Alzheimer's.

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:00.750
And I said to my brother

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:02.040
please can you take a picture of it?

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:03.750
Cause I can't remember
which shoulder she had it on

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:05.100
on her left or right shoulder

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:06.900
because I wanted the
same tattoo for myself.

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:08.100
And he tried to take a picture

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:10.451
of her but she's like, what are you doing?

00:09:10.451 --> 00:09:12.570
And so we struggled to
get for like six months.

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:13.860
And then finally we said, mom

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:15.150
we need a picture of your tattoo.

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:17.100
And she goes, what tattoo?

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:18.122
- Oh.

00:09:18.122 --> 00:09:19.350
- Because she forgot. She forgot.

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:21.390
But we got a picture on the left shoulder

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:22.440
so I got the same thing.

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:23.273
And there we go.

00:09:23.273 --> 00:09:24.390
- That's beautiful though.

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:25.500
Have her with you.

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:26.790
I love that.

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:29.880
So what you got into TikTok last year?

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:31.350
I need to show the world this.

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:34.083
Here's you doing the
whipped cream challenge.

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:37.203
- What?

00:09:38.758 --> 00:09:40.290
- Missed it?

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:41.223
- Merry Christmas.

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:48.117
- Look at the dog. And the dog.

00:09:48.117 --> 00:09:51.690
- The dog. The dog is like,
I love this challenge.

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:52.523
- Are you...

00:09:52.523 --> 00:09:53.356
- Very happy.

00:09:53.356 --> 00:09:54.590
- Are you ou still into TikTok?

00:09:56.580 --> 00:09:58.110
- I'm going to get more into it.

